A||||11||Standard||Anybody who's refinished GT lace wheels..||||5||
Z||000000||Standard||04-10-2000||02:17 AM||||How tough of a job is it?  The clearcoat is peeling really bad on 3 of mine.. and only kind of bad on the 4th  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.  What did you use to strip the wheel with?<P>also, what do they look like under the paint?  I would like to leave them unpainted if possible, just clearcoat them.  thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||04-10-2000||02:30 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com/wheel.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com/wheel.htm</A> <P>hope this helps!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||04-10-2000||03:17 AM||||I'm assuming that's a pic of your rims?  wow.  That's with all the paint stripped off, right?  hard to tell if they're painted or not in the pic.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||04-10-2000||12:18 PM||||Standard, that's my wheel. (Thanks Darklord.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)<BR>I used KS-3 stripper from Home Depot. Really nasty stuff. Would like to find another method of stripping the paint. Something on the order of a hot tank. (Can you hot tank aluminum?)<BR>The wheel in the pic has been repainted with PlastiKote paint. It's not quite as durable as the original. Powder coating may work better. <BR>On the other hand, I'm sure the PlastiKote would be much easier to strip if you decided to change colors.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||wilburt||04-10-2000||02:30 PM||||Just refinished my GT wheels last week.<BR>the hardest thing about the whole job is sanding the aluminum outer edge.  I hand sanded mine with 80 grit, then 150, 300, 400, and finally 600 grit.  Then I masked the sanded part and painted the lace part.  The lace part on some of the wheels is kinda two colors,(after stripping), so you probably will want to paint them.  Each wheel takes about 6 hours, (2 to strip, 4 to sand.)  It's a real pain, but I'd do it again, 'cause they look great! ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||04-10-2000||02:40 PM||||Thanks everyone!  what do you think about black lace wheels on a black car?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eric||04-10-2000||03:06 PM||||I would reccomend not stripping the center part of the wheel.  It just takes too long.  The best thing to do would be to find a sand blaster or media blaster (something that won't ruin the aluminum) and hit the entire wheel with that.  It should remove the clear off the lip and etch the center powder coated section enough to hold paint.  If you strip it completely, you'll find that the underlying aluminum has a grainy rough surface, something you really don't want to sand out.  As far a polishing goes, I would try to find someone who could turn the wheels to clean up the lip.  The sanding is something you don't want to deal with either.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||04-10-2000||08:53 PM||||Has anyone ever tried to sand the lace smooth?  I wonder what that would look like.  I like the "BBS" look, but not the rough texture.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||04-10-2000||10:01 PM||||I have a black Formula with black lace wheels. When the car and wheels are clean, it looks awsome. The only part of the car that stands out is the silver lip and center cap on each wheel. I like it. I really need to redo my wheels.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Eric||04-10-2000||10:17 PM||||Monkeyman, when I described the rough texture of the underlying alumninum as a rough grainy texture, I was really understating it.  The wheel was cast to have a pitted surface to hold the powdercoat.  I don't think you could ever sand the surface smooth.  When I refinished/polished my wheels, my biggest mistake was trying to remove the black paint.  Just spray right over it.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Standard||04-11-2000||12:14 AM||||Monkey,<BR>That's what I wanted to do, but I guess that's outta the question.  grrrr.  I think they would look awesome with bare aluminum laces.. oh well.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||04-11-2000||09:45 AM||||I don't have any GT wheels nor am I planning on getting any in the near future (the Grape Ape already has some 15" aftermarket rims on her), but I just thought it would be a good look.  I've never taken the time to look at any up close, though.  I didn't realize they were made so rough, although anything's possible if you set your mind to it.  Probably too much hassle, frustration, etc right??||||reg||1
